This is the kind of project that will move you from "I know React" to "I can build products."

Perfect. I actually like this PRD enough that we won't need to redesign the product anymore. It is detailed enough to serve as our blueprint.

There are just three things I would add before we start coding:

1. Database Schema (Very Important)

The PRD explains what the app should do, but not exactly how our MongoDB collections relate.

We'll create this ourselves before writing any code.

Users
│
├── Transactions
│      ├── Income
│      └── Expense
│
├── Budgets
│
├── AI Chats
│
└── Categories

We'll design every schema first.

2. API Contract

Before coding the backend we'll decide every endpoint.

Example

POST   /api/auth/register

POST   /api/auth/login

GET    /api/dashboard

GET    /api/transactions

POST   /api/transactions

PATCH  /api/transactions/:id

DELETE /api/transactions/:id

GET    /api/budgets

POST   /api/budgets

POST   /api/assistant/chat

Then we know exactly what the frontend will consume.

3. Folder Architecture

This is the biggest improvement over your Todo project.

Everything should already have a home before we write code.

FinPilot-AI

frontend/
backend/

docs/
    PRD.md
    API.md
    DATABASE.md
Then we follow your development workflow exactly

This is now our permanent workflow for every serious project:

1. Plan
      ↓
2. Design
      ↓
3. Backend
      ↓
4. Frontend
      ↓
5. Connect
      ↓
6. Refactor

We won't jump ahead. If we're building the backend, we stay focused on the backend until it's solid.

Backend structure
backend/

src/

config/
database/

controllers/

routes/

models/

middleware/

services/

utils/

types/

interfaces/

validators/

constants/

app.ts
server.ts
Why this structure?
config/ → environment variables, CORS, JWT config.
database/ → MongoDB connection.
controllers/ → contain business logic.
routes/ → define API endpoints only.
models/ → Mongoose schemas.
middleware/ → authentication, error handling.
services/ → AI service and reusable business logic.
utils/ → helper functions (date formatting, currency formatting, etc.).
validators/ → request validation.
types/interfaces/ → shared TypeScript types.
constants/ → category lists, supported currencies, roles, etc.

This separation keeps the project scalable as it grows.

